#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068377 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "da62e401-d03d-54fd-90b5-410d36bdefad" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1226" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}-3$." :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}{:}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/695bd620f29881bf3d1d12a15a27402a.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}-3\n$$\n\nwe take the graph of\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4}\\right)^{x}\n$$\n\nand shift it $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-2)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/06e822096d671208bbf430caf55e8190.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068378 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "be6b6b07-c139-5ba2-b463-420a2ead54c8" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1227" :question/problem "Plot the graph of $y = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x + 1$." :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/3a95ed2ef7081272671f5a41923a47a2.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $2$. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,2)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/6817eccf2c7e1306200e1729650b093f.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of\n\n$$\ny = 2\\left(\\dfrac{1}{5}\\right)^x + 1\n$$\n\nTo do this, we shift the above graph $1$ unit up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,2)$ to $(0,3)$, and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/9f34b42a63bcf684cb04714ab7ee5292.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068379 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "5ab90279-394b-5df0-8bf3-a1042196b40c" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1228" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y = k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c$. Determine the values of $k$ and $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/d8f79039924340963dd585e4535b185a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve\n\n$$\ny=k\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x}+c\n$$\n\nis $y=c$. In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-5$, hence $c=-5$.\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y$. The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-1)$, so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-1$ into the function and solve for $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} + c &= y \\\\\nk(\\frac{1}{2})^{x} - 5 &= y \\\\\nk(\\frac{1}{2})^{0} - 5 &= -1 \\\\\nk - 5 &= -1 \\\\\nk &= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny = 4\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} - 5\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "20a59278-1d09-5519-a362-4634ac9d5144" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-1587" :question/problem "The graph below shows the curve $y=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^{x} + c$. Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/456/Source/Images/54cd2e1f9633f7e5d5c1536079217c29.png)" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of\n\n$$\ny = \\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x\n$$\n\nis $y = 0$.\n\nThe vertical translation\n\n$$\ny=\\left(\\dfrac{1}{4} \\right)^x+c\n$$\n\nshifts the asymptote to $y=c$.\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=1$. Therefore, $c=1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e48bb5f-f8c0-51a2-9e42-f3a951bedc73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066258} {:answer/id #uuid "16ce6f74-a971-5e51-80f9-906e367f7387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066259} {:answer/id #uuid "215af631-845c-59fa-bb65-f18cb5bf2c8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066260} {:answer/id #uuid "32737268-dd09-57a4-8aeb-64621275384e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066261} {:answer/id #uuid "70266bde-ab79-5c42-abf0-c4d154e65ed8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16ce6f74-a971-5e51-80f9-906e367f7387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066259} :answer-field/id #uuid "69b9ea84-df56-5bb2-b4ac-3db48d048c98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066257}] :question/id #uuid "3dd2a7c3-0fdf-5e47-bc41-0d52827cdf03" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy